# Priority, attribution and source scope

The exact claim is the NP-completeness of the finite threshold problem for one connected simple undirected graph, one selected spanning tree, every vertex root, and explicitly supplied nonnegative integral costs over each whole induced orientation. The paper uses the already audited `B=2` specialization, giving `{0,1,2}`; the positive shift gives `{1,2,3}`. These, transposition and dense encoding are consequences of the same mechanism, not independent novelty claims.

The original primary target is Volker Kaibel, **Open Problem: Arborescences and NP-hardness**, Problem 1, printed pp. 3014–3015 of Oberwolfach Report 50/2018, [DOI 10.4171/OWR/2018/50](https://doi.org/10.4171/OWR/2018/50). The workshop was 4–10 November 2018; the publisher dates the report to 17 December 2019. The entire Kaibel contribution was inspected in pinned primary custody. The adjacent Problem 2 concerns destination paths and supplies no conclusion here.

Kaibel explicitly gives Martin's extension as motivation. This classical formulation is not claimed as new. This preparation independently read the complete spanning-tree section 6.1, pp. 25–26, of Conforti, Cornuéjols and Zambelli's [February 2010 primary exposition](https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/gc0v/webpub/ExtFor-Feb2010.pdf), and Friesen's Proposition 2 and its explanation, p. 8, in her [2019 dissertation](https://d-nb.info/1219966134/34). Their whole survey/thesis were not claimed read. The formulation's common edge variables, nonnegative root-arc variables and indegree constraints identify common-tree integer realizations; projection integrality alone does not settle arbitrary objectives over the full joint lift.

The imported complexity dependency is the standard NP-completeness of satisfiability with at most three literals per clause, cited to [Karp's 1972 paper](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9). Prior mathematical source audits authenticated and inspected that primary dependency. This preparation did not claim a new full read of the scanned Karp paper.

The source/model audit completed 6 October 2026 located no earlier covering theorem or valid exact transfer. Three independently frozen families covered exact-question history, Martin/integer-lift lineage and classical tree mechanisms; a fresh combined adversary assessed them and relevant originals. The complete effective final reports were read for this preparation. Current root adjudication supports a substantive resolution relative to inspected primary literature and clears scoped attributed note preparation. It does not certify absolute first discovery, continuing open status in 2026, or exhaustive historical coverage. Search silence alone is not proof of novelty. A concrete prior exact theorem or checked objective-preserving transfer would reopen the disposition.

The effective classical report is **corrected V2**. Its V1 inference that removing flow charges always gives minimum spanning arborescence was withdrawn: zero-demand vertices can remain optional Steiner vertices. The corrected statement restricts that conclusion to mandatory full support in an independent pricing problem. No checked transfer from that model to this whole-orientation objective was established. V1 is retained elsewhere as history and is not an effective assertion in this package.

Historical access and coverage limits remain explicit:

- Martin's original 1991 proof and 1986/1988 working papers were not retrieved or fully inspected. The [1991 publisher record](https://doi.org/10.1016/0167-6377(91)90028-N) and later exact primary formulations were inspected. This attribution does not represent unread original proofs as read.
- Hu's [1974 original communication-tree paper](https://doi.org/10.1137/0203015) full proof was unavailable; primary metadata and model abstract were inspected. It is a pair-path model, with no authenticated target transfer.
- The original SEA 2011 changeover-cost text was unavailable. Its [revised 2013 author manuscript](https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2011/09